# scripts/privacy-profile — the PUBLICATION tree (github.com/sigiletlabs).
#
# scripts/ci.sh privacy reads this file. It is the ONLY thing that differs
# between this tree and the dev tree: the gate itself is byte-identical, so
# the two cannot drift into checking different things.
#
# There are no `allow` lines, on purpose.
#
# This tree is public. It must contain no private identifier at all, so there is
# nothing here to exempt — not a fixture, not a work log, not a filename. The
# dev tree's profile names two paths that are private on purpose; copying that
# file here would be the mistake this file's emptiness makes obvious in a diff.
#
# Absence would mean the same thing as emptiness: no file, no exemptions. This
# file is committed anyway, because "there is nothing to allow" is a decision
# somebody made, and it should read as one rather than as an oversight.
#
# Grammar, one directive per line, comments with #:
#
#   profile <name>
#   allow <worktree|history> <glob> [<class> ...]
#
#   scope   worktree  the checkout as it stands now
#           history   every commit reachable from any ref, i.e. `git grep` over
#                     `git rev-list --all` — a blob that was once committed
#   class   home     a /home/<user> or /Users/<user> style path
#           shape    one of the built-in shape patterns (tailnet, RFC1918,
#                    an ssh-alias remote, a *.ts.net name)
#           local    one line from $AQM_PRIVACY_PATTERNS, the untracked list of
#                    literal private names
#           Omit the class list and the path is exempt from all three.
#
# Glob: relative to the repository root, `/` separators. `*` stays inside one
# path segment; `**` crosses them.
#
# Override the file with AQM_PRIVACY_PROFILE=<path>.
#
# Do not add an `allow` line without a reason in the commit message that adds
# it. Every line here is a hole in a gate that exists because a leaked blob
# cannot be un-leaked.

profile public